<p>1.Spring framework just started just as an inversion of control containe=
r; the techniques often referred as dependency injection.</p>
<p>2. It was conceived to reduce or replace some complex configurations of =
the earlier Java enterprise edition.</p>
<p>3. Spring,without EJBs, so its original concept was how to work better w=
ith EJBs, and they just realized they didn't need those for many situations=
.</p>
<ol start=3D"4">
<li>
<p>It just transitions into , how can I do the same development without usi=
ng Enterprise Java beans?</p></li>
<li>
<p>Spring is nothing but a dependency injection container.</p></li>
</ol>
<h4 id=3D"SpringFramework6Fundamentals-IOC:-"><br>
IOC:-</h4>
<h4 id=3D"SpringFramework6Fundamentals-InversionofControl(IoC)isadesignprin=
cipleinsoftwaredevelopmentwherethecontrolofobjectcreationandmanagementistra=
nsferredfromtheprogrammertotheframeworkorcontainer.Thisallowstheframeworkto=
managedependenciesandlifecycleofobjects,">Inversion of Control (IoC) is a d=
esign principle in software development where the control of object creatio=
n and management is transferred from the programmer to the framework or con=
tainer. This allows the framework to manage dependencies and lifecycle of o=
bjects, promoting loose coupling between components.</h4>
<p>In simpler terms, instead of creating and managing dependencies manually=
, IoC allows an external entity (like Spring) to handle these tasks, improv=
ing flexibility and testability.</p>
<p>A common way to implement IoC is through <strong>Dependency Injection (D=
I)</strong>, where objects are passed their dependencies rather than creati=
ng them internally.</p>
<p></p>
<p><strong>Dependency Injection (DI)</strong>:-<br>
Dependency Injection (DI) is a way to provide objects with the things they =
need to work (dependencies) instead of making them find or create those thi=
ngs themselves.</p>
<p>For example, imagine a <strong>Car</strong> needs an <strong>Engine</str=
ong> to run. In DI, instead of the <strong>Car</strong> creating its own <s=
trong>Engine</strong>, someone else gives it the <strong>Engine</strong>. T=
his makes the <strong>Car</strong> more flexible because you can easily cha=
nge the <strong>Engine</strong> it uses without changing the <strong>Car</s=
trong> itself.</p>
<p>In simple terms, DI helps in reducing the direct dependency between obje=
cts, making them easier to change and test. The framework (like Spring) aut=
omatically takes care of injecting these dependencies for you.</p>
<p></p>
<h3 id=3D"SpringFramework6Fundamentals-TypesofDependencyInjection:">Types o=
f Dependency Injection:</h3>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p><strong>Constructor Injection</strong>: Dependencies are passed to the o=
bject via its constructor.<br></p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: java; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">public =
class Car {
    private Engine engine;
   =20
    public Car(Engine engine) {
        this.engine =3D engine;
    }
}
</pre>
</div>
</div></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Setter Injection</strong>: Dependencies are provided through set=
ter methods after the object is created.<br></p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: java; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">public =
class Car {
    private Engine engine;
   =20
    public void setEngine(Engine engine) {
        this.engine =3D engine;
    }
}</pre>
</div>
</div></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Interface Injection</strong>: Dependencies are provided through =
an interface, which requires the object to implement certain methods to acc=
ept the dependency.</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: java; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">public =
interface EngineInjector {
    void injectEngine(Engine engine);
}

public class Car implements EngineInjector {
    private Engine engine;

    @Override
    public void injectEngine(Engine engine) {
        this.engine =3D engine;
    }
}</pre>
</div>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p>In <strong>Spring</strong>, DI is commonly used to manage objects and th=
eir relationships, with the framework handling object creation and wiring a=
utomatically through configuration files (XML) or annotations (like <code>@=
Autowired</code>).</p>
<p></p>
<h3 id=3D"SpringFramework6Fundamentals-WhatisSpring:-"><span data-colorid=
=3D"biedwhjc9j">What is Spring:-</span></h3>
<p>It is a framework built around reducing the complexities of enterprise J=
ava development and later providing enterprise development without EJBs. Sp=
ring can essentially be used with or without EJBs and is primarily now used=
 without them.</p>
<div class=3D"confluence-information-macro confluence-information-macro-inf=
ormation">
<span class=3D"aui-icon aui-icon-small aui-iconfont-info confluence-informa=
tion-macro-icon"></span>
<div class=3D"confluence-information-macro-body">
<p>Spring enables us to develop without an application server.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class=3D"panel" style=3D"background-color: #EAE6FF;border-width: 1px;"=
>
<div class=3D"panelContent" style=3D"background-color: #EAE6FF;">
<p>Tomcat is not an application server; it is just a web server. This is on=
e of the reasons that Tomcat has taken over for Java development as a stand=
ard container: It's very easy to use and lightweight. Until spring, you eit=
her weren=E2=80=99t using enterprise features or you had some more complex,=
 harder-to-use application server.<br><br>
Lot of times now we actually use an embedded tomcat instance or jetty insid=
e of a jar , so we don't even realize we=E2=80=99re using tomcat for a web =
server because its embedded in the executable jar.</p>
</div>
</div>
<p><strong>POJO Based:-</strong> Any code you write in Spring can be writte=
n without using Spring at all, POJO. is stands for plain old Java object. S=
pring is just helping us to write code cleaner and better and do things POJ=
O-based and interface-driven.</p>
<p></p>
<p>What problem Spring is trying to solve:-</p><span class=3D"confluence-em=
bedded-file-wrapper image-center-wrapper confluence-embedded-manual-size"><=
img class=3D"confluence-embedded-image image-center" alt=3D"image-20240914-=
064345.png" width=3D"468" loading=3D"lazy" src=3D"480c771e1851b77385eab6bbe=
e40b8fde6b70202d8e730fdf7f232b1fd6feb24" data-image-src=3D"https://thingwor=
x.jira.com/wiki/download/attachments/4181721154/image-20240914-064345.png?v=
ersion=3D1&amp;modificationDate=3D1726296229847&amp;cacheVersion=3D1&amp;ap=
i=3Dv2" data-height=3D"538" data-width=3D"679" data-unresolved-comment-coun=
t=3D"0" data-linked-resource-id=3D"4181721198" data-linked-resource-version=
=3D"1" data-linked-resource-type=3D"attachment" data-linked-resource-defaul=
t-alias=3D"image-20240914-064345.png" data-base-url=3D"https://thingworx.ji=
ra.com/wiki" data-linked-resource-content-type=3D"image/png" data-linked-re=
source-container-id=3D"4181721154" data-linked-resource-container-version=
=3D"21" data-media-id=3D"78ccdbc1-0621-4468-8adc-b1a42cecb89b" data-media-t=
ype=3D"file" height=3D"371"></span>
<p>Q: We understand it reduces the configuration, increases the scalability=
, and increases business focus, but how does that work?</p>
<p>:- 1. It is because everything in the spring is a simple POJO or plain o=
ld Java object.<br>
 2. Spring itself can be thought of as a glorified HasMap; it is a little b=
it more technical than that, but it's actually called the application conte=
xt, and it helps us resolve our resources.</p>
<p>3. The application context is the configured spring container with all o=
f the dependencies wired up in it.</p>
<p></p>
<h2 id=3D"SpringFramework6Fundamentals-SpringConfigurationusingJava"><stron=
g><span data-colorid=3D"en7u7zg0ts">Spring Configuration using Java</span><=
/strong></h2>
<p><strong>Java Configuration</strong> in Spring refers to using Java class=
es instead of XML to configure Spring beans and their dependencies. This ap=
proach is more intuitive and allows for better readability, type safety, an=
d IDE support compared to XML configuration.</p>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p><strong>No applicationContext.xml:</strong> There is no applicationConte=
xt file; we still have the context, but it is not configured using XML.</p>=
</li>
</ol>
<h3 id=3D"SpringFramework6Fundamentals-KeyAnnotationsinJavaConfiguration:">=
Key Annotations in Java Configuration:</h3>
<ol start=3D"3">
<li>
<p><strong>@Configuration</strong>: Marks a class as a configuration class =
that will define beans.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>@Bean</strong>: Indicates that a method will return an object th=
at Spring should manage as a bean.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>@ComponentScan</strong>: Automatically detects and registers bea=
ns from specified packages.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>@Autowired</strong>: Automatically injects dependencies into a b=
ean</p></li>
</ol>
<p>Example of Java Configuration</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: java; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">import =
org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;

@Configuration
public class AppConfig {

    @Bean
    public Car car() {
        return new Car(engine());
    }

    @Bean
    public Engine engine() {
        return new Engine();
    }
}
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<h3 id=3D"SpringFramework6Fundamentals-Explanation:">Explanation:</h3>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>@Configuration</strong>: This tells Spring that this class conta=
ins bean definitions. This annotation is class level annotation.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>@Bean</strong>: The methods annotated with <code>@Bean</code> wi=
ll be executed by Spring to create and manage the returned object as a bean=
.</p></li>
<li>
<p>The <code>car()</code> method returns a <code>Car</code> object, which d=
epends on the <code>engine()</code> method to get an <code>Engine</code> ob=
ject.</p></li>
</ul>
<p>Using the Configuration:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: java; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">import =
org.springframework.context.ApplicationContext;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.AnnotationConfigApplicationCo=
ntext;

public class MainApp {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ApplicationContext context =3D new AnnotationConfigApplicationConte=
xt(AppConfig.class);
       =20
        Car myCar =3D context.getBean(Car.class);
        myCar.start();  // Using the car object with the injected engine
    }
}
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>Benefits of Java Configuration:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Type Safety</strong>: You can work with actual Java objects, cat=
ching errors at compile time.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Readability</strong>: Java code is generally easier to read and =
maintain than large XML configurations.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Refactor-Friendly</strong>: Refactoring tools in IDEs (like rena=
ming a class) work better with Java-based configuration.</p></li>
</ul>
<p>In modern Spring applications, Java-based configuration is the preferred=
 approach due to its flexibility and simplicity.</p>
<h3 id=3D"SpringFramework6Fundamentals-1.ConfigurationAnnotation:-"><br><sp=
an data-colorid=3D"spawzza2sf">1. Configuration Annotation:-</span></h3><sp=
an class=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper image-center-wrapper confluenc=
e-embedded-manual-size"><img class=3D"confluence-embedded-image image-cente=
r" alt=3D"image-20240915-071254.png" width=3D"468" loading=3D"lazy" src=3D"=
