<h2 id=3D"SpringFramework6Fundamentals-@PostConstructAnnotation"><code>@Pos=
tConstruct</code><strong> Annotation</strong></h2>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Purpose:</strong><br>
The <code>@PostConstruct</code> annotation is used to specify a method that=
 should be executed <strong>after</strong> a Spring bean has been fully ini=
tialized, i.e., after dependency injection is complete.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>When to Use:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>To perform any initialization tasks.</p></li>
<li>
<p>To validate that all necessary dependencies are set.</p></li>
<li>
<p>To set up resources or configurations needed by the bean.</p></li>
</ul></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Key Points:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Only one method per bean should be annotated with <code>@PostConstruct</=
code>.</p></li>
<li>
<p>The method must be <code>void</code>, have no arguments, and should not =
throw checked exceptions.</p></li>
<li>
<p>It runs <strong>once</strong> during the bean lifecycle, right after dep=
endencies are injected.</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: java; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">@Servic=
e
public class MyService {

    @PostConstruct
    public void init() {
        // Initialization logic
        System.out.println("MyService has been initialized.");
    }

    public void performAction() {
        System.out.println("Action performed by MyService.");
    }
}
</pre>
</div>
</div></li>
</ul></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>FactoryBean:</strong></p>
<p>In Spring, a <code>FactoryBean</code> is a special type of bean that ser=
ves as a factory for creating other beans. Unlike regular beans, a <code>Fa=
ctoryBean</code> gives you more control over the instantiation process of b=
eans, including the ability to customize the creation logic, manage complex=
 initialization steps, or return objects that are not necessarily tied to t=
he Spring lifecycle.</p>
<p>When you define a <code>FactoryBean</code>, Spring will automatically us=
e it to create objects for you. However, the <code>FactoryBean</code> itsel=
f is not the bean; rather, it is a factory for creating the actual bean ins=
tances. You can control whether the object created by the <code>FactoryBean=
</code> is a singleton or a prototype.</p>
<h3 id=3D"SpringFramework6Fundamentals-KeyMethodsinFactoryBeanInterface:">K=
ey Methods in FactoryBean Interface:</h3>
<p>The <code>FactoryBean</code> interface has three main methods:</p>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p><code>T getObject()</code>: This method returns the instance of the bean=
 that the <code>FactoryBean</code> creates.</p></li>
<li>
<p><code>boolean isSingleton()</code>: This method determines if the bean c=
reated by the <code>FactoryBean</code> is a singleton or not. If it returns=
 <code>true</code>, the bean is a singleton; otherwise, it=E2=80=99s a prot=
otype.</p></li>
<li>
<p><code>Class&lt;?&gt; getObjectType()</code>: This method returns the typ=
e of object that the <code>FactoryBean</code> creates.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 id=3D"SpringFramework6Fundamentals-Example:UsingaFactoryBeantoCreateObj=
ects">Example: Using a FactoryBean to Create Objects</h3>
<p>Let's consider an example where we want to create a bean of <code>Car</c=
ode> using a <code>CarFactoryBean</code>.</p>
<h4 id=3D"SpringFramework6Fundamentals-Step1:DefinetheBean(Car)">Step 1: De=
fine the Bean (Car)</h4>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: java; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">public =
class Car {
    private String model;
    private String make;

    public Car(String model, String make) {
        this.model =3D model;
        this.make =3D make;
    }

    // Getters and setters
    public String getModel() {
        return model;
    }

    public void setModel(String model) {
        this.model =3D model;
    }

    public String getMake() {
        return make;
    }

    public void setMake(String make) {
        this.make =3D make;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "Car{" +
                "model=3D'" + model + '\'' +
                ", make=3D'" + make + '\'' +
                '}';
    }
}
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>Step 2: Implement the FactoryBean (CarFactoryBean)</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: java; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">import =
org.springframework.beans.factory.FactoryBean;

public class CarFactoryBean implements FactoryBean&lt;Car&gt; {
    private String carModel;
    private String carMake;

    public void setCarModel(String carModel) {
        this.carModel =3D carModel;
    }

    public void setCarMake(String carMake) {
        this.carMake =3D carMake;
    }

    @Override
    public Car getObject() throws Exception {
        return new Car(carModel, carMake);
    }

    @Override
    public Class&lt;?&gt; getObjectType() {
        return Car.class;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean isSingleton() {
        return true; // or false depending on your need
    }
}</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>If you're using Java-based configuration (annotations):</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: java; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">@Config=
uration
public class AppConfig {

    @Bean
    public CarFactoryBean carFactoryBean() {
        CarFactoryBean factoryBean =3D new CarFactoryBean();
        factoryBean.setCarModel("Model S");
        factoryBean.setCarMake("Tesla");
        return factoryBean;
    }

    @Bean
    public Car car(CarFactoryBean carFactoryBean) throws Exception {
        return carFactoryBean.getObject();
    }
}
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>Step 4: Using the FactoryBean</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: java; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">public =
class Application {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ApplicationContext context =3D new AnnotationConfigApplicationConte=
xt(AppConfig.class);

        Car car =3D context.getBean("car", Car.class);
        System.out.println(car); // Output: Car{model=3D'Model S', make=3D'=
Tesla'}
    }
}
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<h3 id=3D"SpringFramework6Fundamentals-ImportantPoints:">Important Points:<=
/h3>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p><strong>Direct Access to FactoryBean</strong>: If you need to access the=
 <code>FactoryBean</code> itself (and not the object it creates), you can p=
refix the bean ID with an ampersand (<code>&amp;</code>). For example, to g=
et the <code>CarFactoryBean</code>, you can call:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: java; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">CarFact=
oryBean factory =3D context.getBean("&amp;carFactory", CarFactoryBean.class=
);</pre>
</div>
</div></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Singleton vs Prototype</strong>: By overriding the <code>isSingl=
eton()</code> method, you can control whether the object created by the <co=
de>FactoryBean</code> is a singleton or prototype. If <code>isSingleton()</=
code> returns <code>true</code>, Spring will create only one instance and r=
eturn it for every request for that bean. If <code>isSingleton()</code> ret=
urns <code>false</code>, a new object will be created every time the bean i=
s requested.</p></li>
</ol>
<h3 id=3D"SpringFramework6Fundamentals-UseCasesforFactoryBean:">Use Cases f=
or FactoryBean:</h3>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>When creating objects that require complex setup</strong>: Somet=
imes, creating a bean involves complex initialization that=E2=80=99s better=
 encapsulated in a separate factory class.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>For integrating third-party libraries</strong>: You may need to =
create objects that are not directly managed by Spring, such as objects fro=
m third-party libraries.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Proxy objects or dynamic creation</strong>: You can use <code>Fa=
ctoryBean</code> to return proxy objects, which are common in AOP or transa=
ction management.</p></li>
</ul>
<p><code>FactoryBean</code> provides a lot of flexibility, especially when =
working with third-party libraries or when bean creation logic is non-trivi=
al.</p>
<h3 id=3D"SpringFramework6Fundamentals-SpringProfiles:-"><strong>Spring Pro=
files:-</strong></h3>
<h3 id=3D"SpringFramework6Fundamentals-SpringProfilesisafeatureoftheSpringF=
rameworkthatallowsyoutodefinedifferentconfigurationsfordifferentenvironment=
s,suchasdevelopment,testing,staging,andproduction.Ithelpstomanageandsegrega=
tebeandefinitionsandconfigurationsfordif"><strong>Spring Profiles</strong> =
is a feature of the Spring Framework that allows you to define different co=
nfigurations for different environments, such as <strong>development</stron=
g>, <strong>testing</strong>, <strong>staging</strong>, and <strong>product=
ion</strong>. It helps to manage and segregate bean definitions and configu=
rations for different environments without altering the source code.</h3>
<p>You can mark beans or configurations with specific profiles, and Spring =
will activate only those beans or configurations that match the active prof=
ile.</p>
<h3 id=3D"SpringFramework6Fundamentals-WhyUseSpringProfiles?">Why Use Sprin=
g Profiles?</h3>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Environment-specific configurations</strong>: For example, in de=
velopment, you might want to use an embedded database, but in production, y=
ou=E2=80=99ll use a remote database.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Selective Bean Registration</strong>: You can define beans that =
should only be created when a particular profile is active.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Externalize Configurations</strong>: Profiles allow externalized=
, environment-specific configurations, which makes your application more po=
rtable.</p></li>
</ul>
<h3 id=3D"SpringFramework6Fundamentals-HowSpringProfilesWork">How Spring Pr=
ofiles Work</h3>
<p>You can assign profiles at the <strong>class level</strong> or <strong>b=
ean level</strong>. You then <strong>activate</strong> specific profiles at=
 runtime, either programmatically or by setting system properties or enviro=
nment variables.</p>
<h3 id=3D"SpringFramework6Fundamentals-Example:BeanProfilesinSpring">Exampl=
e: Bean Profiles in Spring</h3>
<h4 id=3D"SpringFramework6Fundamentals-1.DefineBeansforDifferentProfiles">1=
. Define Beans for Different Profiles</h4>
<p>Suppose you have a <code>DataSource</code> bean that should behave diffe=
rently in <strong>development</strong> and <strong>production</strong> envi=
ronments.</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: java; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">@Config=
uration
@Profile("dev")
public class DevDataSource {

    @Bean
    public DataSource dataSource() {
        System.out.println("Development DataSource bean created!");
        // Return a DataSource for development, e.g., an H2 embedded databa=
se
        return new EmbeddedDatabaseBuilder()
                .setType(EmbeddedDatabaseType.H2)
                .build();
    }
}
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<h4 id=3D"SpringFramework6Fundamentals-2.ActivatingProfiles">2. Activating =
Profiles</h4>
<p>You can activate a specific profile in multiple ways.</p>
<h5 id=3D"SpringFramework6Fundamentals-a.Programmatically(incode)">a. Progr=
ammatically (in code)</h5>
<p>You can activate a profile programmatically when creating the applicatio=
n context:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: java; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">public =
class Application {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        AnnotationConfigApplicationContext context =3D new AnnotationConfig=
ApplicationContext();
        context.getEnvironment().setActiveProfiles("dev");  // Set "dev" pr=
ofile
        context.register(AppConfig.class);
        context.refresh();

        DataSource dataSource =3D context.getBean(DataSource.class);
        System.out.println(dataSource);
        context.close();
    }
}
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>You can activate a profile by setting an environment variable. For examp=
le, when starting your Spring Boot application, you can pass the active pro=
file as a parameter:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: java; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">$ java =
-jar myapp.jar --spring.profiles.active=3Dprod</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p></p>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
